# MO — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; `.env.example` must never
# contain a real value.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything prefixed VITE_ is compiled into the JavaScript bundle and is PUBLIC.
# Every visitor can read it with devtools. That is fine for the two values
# below -- the anon key is designed to be public, and row-level security is what
# actually protects your data.
#
# The service role key is the opposite. It bypasses RLS entirely. It must only
# ever exist in the worker's environment, never in this app, never behind a
# VITE_ prefix, never in the browser.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === Frontend (public) =====================================================

# Supabase project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijkl.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=

# Supabase "anon" / publishable key. Public by design.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Public base URL for approved photos, served from the Cloudflare-proxied
# R2 hostname, e.g. https://img.example.com
# Must be behind Cloudflare for the free CSAM scanning tool to apply.
#
# The upload endpoint refuses to sign anything while this is unset, even though
# it never uses the value. Accepting photos with nowhere to serve them from
# means an unscanned bucket, a map with no pictures, and admins asked to judge
# images they cannot see.
VITE_PHOTO_BASE_URL=

# Where the browser loads the NSFW check model from, the first time somebody
# picks a photo. Left unset it uses a public CDN.
#
# This is deliberately NOT a bundled dependency: installing nsfwjs normally
# pulls TensorFlow and the model weights into the build, measured at 39.6 MB of
# output versus 0.85 MB without. Point this at your own copy to avoid depending
# on a CDN.
VITE_NSFW_MODULE_URL=


# === Worker (secret — belongs in worker/.env, not here) ====================

# Same project URL as above.
SUPABASE_URL=

# SERVICE ROLE KEY. Bypasses row-level security completely.
# Never commit this. Never expose it to a browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Where the moderation models run. Any OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint works,
# which covers Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp and the hosted providers.
# Switching machines is this one line.
#   http://192.168.1.40:11434/v1     -- Ollama on your PC
#   http://homeserver.lan:8000/v1    -- vLLM on a local server
#   https://api.openai.com/v1        -- hosted, if you ever want it
MODERATION_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:11434/v1

# Only set if your endpoint requires it. Local hosts usually do not.
MODERATION_API_KEY=

# Tier 3 models. No specific model is a design commitment; the tiers are
# defined by role, so swapping model is just a name change here.
MODERATION_TEXT_MODEL=qwen3:8b
MODERATION_VISION_MODEL=qwen2.5vl:7b

# Tier 2 thresholds. Scores outside these bands are auto-decided; only the
# band between them reaches a human. Widen the bands if your review queue is
# noisier than you want.
NSFW_AUTO_REJECT_ABOVE=0.85
NSFW_AUTO_APPROVE_BELOW=0.15
TOXICITY_AUTO_REJECT_ABOVE=0.80
TOXICITY_AUTO_APPROVE_BELOW=0.20

# Worker loop
WORKER_ID=local-dev
WORKER_BATCH_SIZE=10
WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=15000


# === Upload signing endpoint (api/sign-upload) =============================
#
# Server-side only. These are read by the serverless function, never by the
# bundle, so they carry no VITE_ prefix and never reach a browser.
#
# The endpoint checks the caller's access token with Supabase before it signs
# anything, which is why it needs the project URL and the anon key as well as
# the R2 credentials. Both fall back to the VITE_ values above if unset, so one
# pair can serve the bundle and the function -- but if you set them here, set
# them to the SAME project. A function pointed at a different project rejects
# every real token, and the symptom is a sign-in that works everywhere except
# uploads.
#
# SEVEN values in total, and the endpoint refuses to sign anything until every
# one of them is set:
#
#   SUPABASE_URL          -- the worker section below sets it; one value serves both
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY     -- here
#   VITE_PHOTO_BASE_URL   -- the FRONTEND section above; see the note below
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID         -- the R2 section below
#   R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID      -- the R2 section below
#   R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  -- the R2 section below
#   R2_BUCKET             -- the R2 section below
#
# Nothing is repeated here, on purpose. A name that appears twice in one file
# takes its LAST value, so a second empty `SUPABASE_URL=` would quietly override
# the one somebody had filled in above.
#
# VITE_PHOTO_BASE_URL is the odd one out, and there is no non-VITE_ spelling of
# it. The endpoint does not use that value; it refuses to sign until it is set,
# on the BUNDLE's behalf, and the bundle reads that exact name. Accepting a
# `PHOTO_BASE_URL` alias here meant the check could pass on a value the app
# would never see: every upload succeeded and every photo came back as "being
# checked" forever, with an admin queue that had nothing to show.
#
# Both URLs must be real https URLs, not bare hostnames. `img.example.com` with
# no scheme gets concatenated into `img.example.com/<key>`, which a browser
# resolves against the app's own origin -- a broken image the UI treats as a
# real photo. The endpoint refuses to sign rather than let that happen.

SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=


# === Cloudflare R2 (secret — worker / upload signer only) ==================
#
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID is the account id, NOT the bucket hostname: the endpoint signs
# against <account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, R2's S3-compatible API.
# Approved photos are then SERVED from VITE_PHOTO_BASE_URL, which is a
# different hostname and the one that has to sit behind Cloudflare.
#
# The bucket is wearechintu's `chintubucket`, shared with the marketplace.
#
# The upload endpoint needs a token that can PUT; the worker needs one that
# can DELETE, for removing rejected and unused photos. The worker takes all
# four of these or none -- some but not all stops it at start-up, since it
# would look configured and delete nothing. Leave all four blank to run the
# worker without the cleanup.
#
# The bucket needs a CORS rule allowing PUT from the site's own origin, or
# every upload fails in the browser before it reaches R2. See api/README.md.

R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
